      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, framing the era as a trade-off between short-term market expansion and long-term social stability is an increasingly accurate and widely accepted perspective for a modern literature review. Most contemporary scholars agree that while policies successfully drove aggregate growth in the initial decades, they often sacrificed equity and systemic resilience for those gains. Acknowledging this fundamental tension positions your argument within current dominant scholarship and avoids the false dichotomy of \"progress vs. stagnation.\" It is a strong approach because it aligns with the nuance that market efficiency (short term) and social equity (long term) can be economically distinct and sometimes conflicting objectives.",
